ille has retired to run for governor. Congressman Barry Moore won a Trump-backed GOP runoff and enters the general election with a huge financial advantage, including roughly $16.5 million in outside support and a presidential rally planned in Mobile. Democrat Everett Wess, a nominee with minimal funding, is campaigning against long odds in a state with a 15-point Republican lean.\n\nEvery major race rater calls the contest Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give the Democrat only a 2-3 percent chance. The one available poll, from July, showed Moore ahead by 15 points. A bribery-related headline about Moore is the only notable wrinkle, but nothing suggests it threatens the expected outcome in this deeply red state.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0155, "margin": -14.03}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.004, "margin": -19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.018, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0262, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0053, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.011, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0049, "margin": -18.0}]}